#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208271 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/id #uuid "a06524d0-6980-52eb-b5fb-2ca7d0c1dbac" :question/math-academy-id "e-17350" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by ![](images/47/47c73d44f28380c2285b914445d6e4eed32e20e4304f8373b0e650d238497569.svg) with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at ![](images/06/0648ff5db79fc2b00307e99fc1b14ddbc277982db84205f281f567a9c39b1eeb.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Writing ![](images/f6/f6bba4e49e37c8115f3d5696f3763224ae83da30fa92e295da8ae2905b6cfe9a.svg), we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = 2A\\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n![](images/b2/b21fb5c13d179b77b77ba08ccd62b4e7f47274fac9fc9ea8c39936a9e52ddcef.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208272 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c77271f-3d21-5193-a1ad-5c0264e337f0" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Gradients"}] :question/id #uuid "fce0099e-b010-50a4-bf0d-51aca4380129" :question/math-academy-id "e-17865" :question/problem "Consider the function $L: \\mathbb{R}^{3} \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})^T(\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b})$ where \n![](images/38/38fae06690f00ec57f1c56f5b3c0f4add88d5505a991f7cbd09020364c973238.svg)\nWhat is $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} L(\\mathbf{x})$ at ![](images/62/6267a6f3c8df9f11cfa395a92d426ab1901e9048cadc6b50640fc5aafc0b59df.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}.$ Then, $L(\\mathbf{x}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y},$ and\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} L = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}L}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}\\right)^T = \n\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}\\right)^T \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}L}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}\\right)^T.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{y}^T\\mathbf{y}\\right) = \\mathbf{y}^T \n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}) = \\mathbf{A},\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ![](images/41/41633d52787dbf98d039bf414f07a7052fe0acf0b55c831bbaf24868b22e95df.svg), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 81 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2198cf08-3a99-5e5d-80af-bbe722b51647" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b4e84c40-15d1-585f-84c7-c615979cd8f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18713" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by\n![](images/9e/9e4b8dccd90c15d35317eb418a99dde9c43f12b8b2780e35292ecc291c10e92d.svg)\nwith respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at  ![](images/37/37e7e5c54e3e7990b627649376f8f55a27db8a82b42b2375f99941793c92ff26.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n![](images/71/7135a764f77153e7753bd3ab87305f8347207f12d87533565c0018588e00a00f.svg)\nwe have $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = (A^T + A)\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n![](images/e5/e5e95b87bd905379b447b17e1ba8946579b85b6ab29831912c154591d5d7bde4.svg)\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at ![](images/5b/5b3d05513f25be9f2d0cc61ba21dbf3b34916f6c2fce1babc8fb4de1627171da.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0 \\\\ 0-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6461005e-b2cf-5ad6-b2a4-f92c5ca3eeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205315} {:answer/id #uuid "1a124c13-f554-5359-962c-90f7cd894e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531988} {:answer/id #uuid "23346f1c-daf0-53aa-8167-87d06065ca2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532008} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6596af-03f0-55a8-b320-a0ebb705a39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532218} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fcfb1c-90f2-5d7d-a157-d8f5b54b8b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1fcfb1c-90f2-5d7d-a157-d8f5b54b8b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532416} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7870c7-9027-5736-8a58-e3aa9dcf3a06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186205310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "beb58517-f82f-57ef-8375-4c1fddab7c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-264509" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5de37790-75ca-5675-aae7-0ff1b0d1659e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532141} {:answer/id #uuid "7b296bdc-8919-5ab6-827d-d951a9c46e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532208} {:answer/id #uuid "87e4df02-a07b-51ac-ba3c-6c80acabaddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532243} {:answer/id #uuid "b1503f40-d92c-57b6-99d1-6c5dcdf41eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532325} {:answer/id #uuid "c4108243-e1bd-51f1-bef8-13c020e0ba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de37790-75ca-5675-aae7-0ff1b0d1659e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532141} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd1b93f4-6b48-5f90-af0f-e979b93d3583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66d888b2-986e-58dd-b75b-3ffbf568a4ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-265764" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "617e9b74-7b40-5151-b5f7-d2c38f23b567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532149} {:answer/id #uuid "c6039c55-9dd5-533f-aad5-b746c73a3126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532361} {:answer/id #uuid "cf529f56-e92e-5984-9cc7-844c47ce8fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532379} {:answer/id #uuid "db817b06-48e6-5db7-99e3-d3b7d92b252b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532406} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c538f5-e615-5347-ac75-743588bef9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c538f5-e615-5347-ac75-743588bef9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532451} :answer-field/id #uuid "81864079-1883-5b8b-8d59-ef9b83084c06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81dfe0ee-3be8-54e9-80f8-e08132e662d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-265766" :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48b8d3bd-6a11-58e6-8539-d23621262558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604