gin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+1+1 \\\\ 1+2-2 \\\\ 1-2+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
